  "meta": {
    "title": "Oil Slick Overlap: The Marine Snow of a Tanker Spill",
    "date": "2026-09-27",
    "lesson": "When a surface oil slick begins to sink as marine oil snow, it drags a lipid-rich particle field down through the water column, and the bacterial community that greets it stratifies by depth just like the light and oxygen do. Near the sunlit surface, alkane-degrading, lipid-specialist cells race collagen- and chitin-users for the fastest-decaying droplets; deeper down, where oxygen thins and nutrients pool, the same droplets persist longer and reward patience over speed. This level asks you to specialize in a wax-ester esterase (a re-skinned lipid enzyme) to break down oil-flocculated particles, while surviving grazers and phage pressure that track your population as it blooms on the windfall of carbon. The lesson: an oil spill is not just a toxin event, it is a pulse of food that reorganizes an entire microbial food web around its slow sinking and decay.",
    "citation": "Passow & Ziervogel (2016), Marine oil snow sedimentation and flocculent accumulation (MOSSFA) after Deepwater Horizon",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "2010 Deepwater Horizon spill, Gulf of Mexico - oil-snow sedimentation and depth-stratified hydrocarbon-degrading bacteria"
  },
